) \u2013 i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is similar to the typical psychology paper (median shown for reference). Relative to other words, <span  \"><i>\"temporally\" </i></span> papers are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">61<sup>st</sup></span> percentile in terms of fragile p-value percentage (lower = better). Note, p-values highly predict replicability (see <a href=\"../assets/pdf/Bogdan_2025_PsyChange_Manuscript_SuppMat.pdf\">the associated paper</a>).","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"temporally\" </i></span> tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">78<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  and tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">59<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is positively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are more likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"temporally\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">98<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "}